1 OCCU Editor and Publisher of Tacoma Tidende
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "John Blaauw was born in Bergen, Norway, 1868, but when an infant of two years he went with his parents to Christiania, where he resided till he reached the age of seventeen, save two and a half years he spent in E
2 CONC dinburgh, Scotland. In 1887 he emigrated to America, making Seattle his destination. Right after the great fire he embarked in Scandinavian journalism, and has practically followed it ever since.
2 CONT
2 CONT No man is better informed of the condition and natural resources on the Pacific coast than Editor John Blaauw. He has traveled in every county from British Columbia to the Golden State. He has made Tacoma Tidende an honor to the c
2 CONC oast and a valuable source of information to thousands of people throughout the United States. He is of a genial disposition, kind and serviceable. Though always busy, yet glad to receive visitors, and willing to impart all possib
2 CONC le knowledge. Much of his time is consumed in replying to letters concerning the Pacific country."
2 CONT
2 CONT "http://www.hellenicaworld.com/USA/Literature/ThomasOstensonStine/en/ScandinaviansOnThePacific.html?fbclid=IwAR06VkV1qmS3qgz0FCG_39bJZTlIfNDe-CzqkgNsRGH9r2sBp6rUg8Dpxg4#CHAPTER_VI
2 CONT https://www.hellenicaworld.com/USA/Literature/ThomasOstensonStine/en/ScandinaviansOnThePacific.html?fbclid=IwAR06VkV1qmS3qgz0FCG_39bJZTlIfNDe-CzqkgNsRGH9r2sBp6rUg8Dpxg4#CHAPTER_VI#129
2 CONT https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42384/pg42384-images.html#Page_129
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Scandinavian press, of Tacoma, is growing into popularity. The first Scandinavian newspaper on record in the city was, "Tacoma Budstikken," a Norwegian-Danish weekly, founded in December, 1899, by P. O. Bergan, but enjoyed on
2 CONC ly a short period of sunshine. The Tacoma Tidende was launched July 5, 1890, and ripened into a Norwegian-Danish state paper. From infancy it was in the hands of Dirk Blaauw ( https://www.geni.com/people/Dirck-Blaauw/6000000018638
2 CONC 377685?through=6000000112372814869) who bid fair at journalism, but a year ago it was transferred to his brother John who has steeped it with journalistic fire, comparing in merit with the big eastern weeklies. It takes a man of a
2 CONC  congenial nature, ability and "push" to make journalism a success, and these qualities manifest themselves every day in the editor of Tacoma Tidende."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Tacoma Tidende'''
2 CONT
2 CONT "Name of creator
2 CONT Tacoma Tidende Publishing Company
2 CONT (1890-1931)
2 CONT Administrative history
2 CONT The Tacoma Tidende Publishing Company was founded in 1890 by Dirk Blaauw with support from the local Scandinavian business community. The first issue of the Tidende newspaper was published July 4, 1890. Blaauw sought to compete wi
2 CONC th Seattle's Washington Posten and to expand readership beyond the City of Tacoma. The paper began advertising itself as "The most widely circulating organ of the Scandinavians in the states of Washington and Oregon, in Alaska and
2 CONC  in British Columbia" and expanded its news coverage and beyond Washington. In 1897, Dirk Blaauw sold the paper to John Blaauw, (Johan Blaauw) who was working as a representative for the paper in Portland, Oregon. He quickly expan
2 CONC ded the number of pages, subscribers, and advertisers and piloted unique cooperative programs with other Scandinavian language publications in the Midwest. While the paper had always been known as the Tacoma Tidende, the title pag
2 CONC e began reflecting this by 1900. Blaauw began directly competing with the Washington Posten by marketing the newspaper and expanding subscribers in Seattle and Ballard. The Tacoma Tidende positioned itself as the more conservative
2 CONC  of the Puget Sound Scandinavian newspapers by voicing its support for temperance and other political and social issues. Blaauw left the newspaper in 1911 and Rudolf Blom Anderson, who had previously worked for the Washington Post
2 CONC en, followed as Publisher and Editor. In 1920, John Soley purchased the newspaper and began to refocus the paper on local news within the Scandinavian community. The paper's name was changed to Vestkysten (The West Coast), which h
2 CONC ad been the title of a short-lived local publication, in 1925. In 1931, Soley sold Vestkysten to The Western Viking."
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archives.plu.edu/index.php/tacoma-tidende-2
2 CONT
