1 NOTE {geni:about_me} EREDETILEG  keresztneve  GUILBRAND,  így ismert szinte világszerte  -  de síremlékén a budapesti u.n. Kerepesi temetőben  Gudbrand  a felirat  (felesége neveként is) Tizenkét gyermeke közül a legkisebb (Endre?) vez
2 CONC ette a norvég konzulátust Budapesten 1930-ig.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://nbl.snl.no/Gudbrand_Gregersen_Sa%C3%A1gi Translated into English States he was a ........
2 CONT
2 CONT Norwegian-Hungarian entrepreneur. Parents: Farmer Niels Gregersen Tyrholm (1804-1868) and Anne Trulsdatter (1803-1838). Married 17/07/1852 in Esztergom, Hungary Luiz (Aluiza) Sümeg (2.9.1836-9.12.1906), daughter of a butcher Josep
2 CONC h Sümeg and Katharina Stitz.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a young craftsman, he went to Hungary, built the country's largest construction company and remained there for the rest of his life. For his efforts with the flood protection of the town Szeged, he was ennobled by Emperor Franz
2 CONC  Joseph in 1884.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born on his father's farm Strand on Modum, but shortly after the family moved to his mother's family farm Flannum. Later Flannum was sold and Gudbrand got 400 spesidaler to resign all rights and used the money for further e
2 CONC ducation. He was living with his father's wealthy cousin, mayor and later MP Auen Gregersen on Mæhlum.
2 CONT
2 CONT After graduation he went to Christiania, where he was apprenticed to builder C. Malling. As an apprentice he was instrumental in the construction of the Royal Palace, while he took private lessons in geometry and mathematics, and 
2 CONC became a student at the Royal School. In 1842 he went to Copenhagen, where he qualified in his trade.  At the same time he also attended the art academy, where he modeled and designed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregersen - or GG, as he often was called - was very industrious and thrifty.  In 1847 he went to Vienna and after some misfortunes, he arrived at Pest in Hungary. There he met the Italian engineer Talagini, who was involved in th
2 CONC e  construction of the country's railways and together they created a railway over the River Vah (in present day Slovenia).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1848 rebellion broke out in Hungary, which was in personal union with Austria. The revolt failed, partly because Austria had help from Russia, and partly because the Hungarians were an ethnically diverse society, some of whom w
2 CONC ere not willing to give equal rights to other ethnic groups.  The Gregersens participation in the uprising is rather unclear, but there are indications that he was an engineer with more than a soldier. After the defeat he went to 
2 CONC Italy, but returned about1850.   Some sources say he received a death sentence for his participation and that he was rescued by a Chief Engineer on the Pest - Vienna underground, but even he stated many years later that he came ba
2 CONC ck because it was given amnesty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregersen then settled in the city Szob the Danube, close to the border with Slovakia where he started his own construction company. In 1865 he moved the entire business to Budapest, but also bought a saw mill in Szob. In 1866 he 
2 CONC moved into Pest and the large house he had in Szob remained as a summer residence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregersen was famed as the builder of dams at the town Szeged, the second largest city in Hungary. In the spring of 1879, the Tisza river broke through the old levee, destroying the city and drowning a large number of people. Greg
2 CONC ersen undertook to pump away the water and then built three dams around the city.  The longest was 15 km long and 9.5 meters high. There was another flood the following year, but the dams held and the disaster did not materialize.
2 CONC  As a reward for this effort, in 1884-1895 Gregersen and his family joined the Hungarian nobility and he was appointed a Knight 1st Class of the Order of St. Olav.
2 CONT
2 CONT G. Gregersen & Son received several major contracts throughout Hungary. One of the major tasks was the construction of the track number. 2 on a 103 km long railway line. The contract was signed on 1 June 1888 was to be completed b
2 CONC y 1 December of the same year, ie a construction period of only six months. The work included the relocation of more than 700,000 m 3 of soil, construction of a four span bridge over the river Ondova and numerous retaining walls. 
2 CONC 200 rail cars and six locomotives were used for transportation on track which was still being used for normal traffic, and work was frequently disrupted. However, on November 28, two days before the deadline, the task was complete
2 CONC d.   It was made possible because of the Gregersens ability to organize thousands of workers to build at many locations simultaneously. He also modernized port facilities in Fiume (now Rijeka in Slovenia).
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregersen's company became the largest construction firm in Hungary. It also established a branch in Prague, managed by two of his sons, György and Ödön. In 1890, the Charles Bridge in Prague (from the 1400s) was heavily damaged b
2 CONC y a flood and Gregersen was commissioned to restore it. The firm also built three other bridges in Prague. Many of the railways, port facilities, dams and buildings Gregersen built, still exist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregersen visited Norway 15 times, and in 1901 he bought the farm Brevik in Modum. In 1902 he became an honorary member of the Norwegian Engineering and Architectural Association. He always retained his Norwegian citizenship.
2 CONT
2 CONT German was the official language of Hungary so Gregersen did not learn much Hungarian, using German in his business. He also held on to his Lutheran faith. In 1852 he married the butcher's daughter Luiza Sümeg and they had 19 chil
2 CONC dren, of whom seven died young.  The 12 who grew up lived in Hungary as Hungarian citizens. Many of Gregersens relatives from Norway also came to Hungary and got work with him, and some of their descendants still remain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudbrand Gregersen died Christmas Eve 1910 at his country house in Szob, when he was 87 years old. His coffin was taken to the family mausoleum in Budapest, where 24 other family members are buried. His heirs created a trust "for 
2 CONC the promotion of technical conception and practice" to enable both men and women to study abroad.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
